{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"b0108f24-43a4-413e-94c9-bd361b6fafc5","hash":"37242c0241657d739ecda534c2355451258021575a403dae68391c19cbb292ad","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Both facts are recorded on `main` and come back from `recall`. Adding the `-race` lesson to the protocol and telling the two agents still running:"},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016zAVCTxTDE1xuzxUveb5YF","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'EOF'\np='/Users/suhaan/devenv-worktrees/timing/G3-PROTOCOL.md'\ns=open(p).read()\na=\"- **No full local suites.**\"\nassert s.count(a)==1\ns=s.replace(a,\"- **CI runs tests with `-race`.** Run your focused tests with `-race` too, under mlock, before trusting a local green. Watch for anything that reads a subprocess's captured `Stdout`/`Stderr` (a `bytes.Buffer` or `strings.Builder`) before `cmd.Wait()`: os/exec copies into it from its own goroutine, so that is a data race.\\n- If CI shows a broad cascade of unrelated failures, **grep the log for `DATA RACE` first.** One race poisons the whole run and looks exactly like resource exhaustion.\\n\"+a)\nopen(p,'w').write(s); print('ok')\nEOF","description":"Add the race-detector rule to the protocol"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01AGHFfrjDFVVr251hdiLmnm","name":"SendMessage","input":{"to":"aead2ee30e4f0404a","summary":"Run -race locally; subprocess-output race shape","message":"One more protocol rule, now in G3-PROTOCOL.md. **CI runs tests with `-race`.** Run your focused tests with `-race` too, under mlock, before trusting a local green.\n\nYour change touches git subprocess environments, so check for this bug shape, which the Brain session just shipped and fixed: `cmd.Stderr = &buf` (a `bytes.Buffer` or `strings.Builder`) read before `cmd.Wait()`. `os/exec` copies into a non-`*os.File` sink from its own goroutine, so that read is a data race. The fake-git env test and any probe that reads stderr early are the likely spots.\n\nIf CI shows a broad cascade of unrelated failures, grep the log for `DATA RACE` before assuming resource exhaustion."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018tK8s2vxc821eShqMYdBQG","name":"SendMessage","input":{"to":"aed163711fd7846a7","summary":"CI cascade → grep DATA RACE first","message":"FYI, a new rule in G3-PROTOCOL.md. CI runs `-race`; you're already running it per your brief, which is good. If CI shows a broad cascade of unrelated test failures, grep the log for `DATA RACE` before assuming resource exhaustion or flakiness, because one race poisons the whole run."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01MS5wRBxQKoPCQVJ473zFac","name":"SendMessage","input":{"to":"fix-brain-initialization-ux","summary":"Facts recorded on main; -race rule added","message":"Done:\n- **Facts recorded on `main`** in entire-graph's brain. I was standing on main, so there's no fork-copy trap.\n  - `fact:d92968bf3732c8b6d18ce5fe`: the NFD/NFC pathspec constraint, `constraints.git.gotcha`. `recall --branch main` returns it at rank 1.\n  - `fact:735ad2c887e8eb05c8ff2c8a`: flock missing, use mlock (`workflow.tooling.gotcha`).\n- **`-race`:** now a protocol rule for every G3 agent. Run focused tests with `-race` locally, watch for subprocess stderr read before `Wait`, and on a CI cascade grep `DATA RACE` before blaming resources. The #372 agent got your exact bug shape, since it touches git subprocess environments.\n\n#371 CI: 22 green, with the macOS and Windows test shards still running and nothing failed so far. #372's binary ping is still held until its matrix and `-race` are confirmed."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
