{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"b0108f24-43a4-413e-94c9-bd361b6fafc5","hash":"8aefeaa61606953672e0e4d881391f6e3de4e9fbd5fd6099fdb315043225e115","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016v375P8hE94DnxvbDeeDZw","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'EOF'\np='/Users/suhaan/devenv-worktrees/timing/G3-PROTOCOL.md'\ns=open(p).read()\na=\"- A test that cannot go red is not a test.\"\nassert s.count(a)==1\ns=s.replace(a,a+\"\"\"\n- **Fake subprocesses that answer and exit must drain stdin** (`cat >/dev/null &` … `wait`). Otherwise the caller's write to stdin races the exit and fails with EPIPE (\"broken pipe\") on CI while passing locally. Fake `git` used with `grep -f -` is exactly this shape.\n- **When a guard depends on goroutine timing, make the fixture force the order.** For example, a fake that closes stdout first, sleeps, then writes its reason. Don't hope the race lands the same way on CI: on macOS one side may win every time, so the weakening passes locally and only CI disagrees.\"\"\")\nopen(p,'w').write(s); print('ok')\nEOF","description":"Add fake-subprocess and race-forcing rules to protocol"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014dKNh7zaAzfdmsfYcaDZad","name":"SendMessage","input":{"to":"fix-brain-initialization-ux","summary":"Both harness traps added to G3 protocol","message":"Both traps are now rules in G3-PROTOCOL.md:\n- fakes that answer and exit must drain stdin and `wait`;\n- when a guard depends on goroutine timing, the fixture forces the order rather than hoping CI's race lands like macOS's.\n\nThe first one maps directly onto our code: the alias grep feeds patterns on stdin (`grep -f -`), so any fake `git` on that path that exits without reading is your EPIPE shape. #371 and #372 are both green in CI, but future agents will get the rule up front. Inverting the race from the fake's side, so the weakening fails locally with the real symptom, is the clever part.\n\nYour sequencing is fine: #477 green first, then the read-only `status` check under both vars, then refresh. No rush from my side."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
