{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"b0108f24-43a4-413e-94c9-bd361b6fafc5","hash":"49edbc8133a8c2cf86f54466d95140b24cd1d54d8d779e474fc0ab6ddd4d5150","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01NJvt3rghxNhL7ekexcAyok","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/suhaan/devenv/entire-graph && entire brain memories retract --help 2>&1 | grep -E 'Usage|--' | head -6; entire brain memories retract fact:0e3ce1673cd4981e07318b7d --branch main --json 2>&1 | tail -3; entire brain remember \"entire-graph may admit partial clones (promisor remotes, e.g. blob:none) only when the runtime Git is >= 2.45.1, NOT 2.45.0. The GIT_NO_LAZY_FETCH early return in promisor-remote.c fetch_objects() arrived in commit 7b70e9efb18c via the 2024-04 security releases (2.39.4, 2.40.2, 2.41.1, 2.42.2, 2.43.4, 2.44.1, 2.45.1); in 2.45.0 the variable only sets fetch_if_missing=0, and rename-detection prefetch (diffcore-rename.c, diff.c) still spawns 'git fetch' (reproduced with git 2.45.0 built from source). GIT_NO_LAZY_FETCH=1 is the load-bearing guard on >=2.45.1; GIT_ALLOW_PROTOCOL= is a second layer (fetch attempted then refused; on Git for Windows the transport stats a UNC URL before the allowlist check, so it is not sufficient alone). Probe the Git version in the repo's own cwd (shims like asdf/mise are cwd-sensitive). Avoid 'git show -s' on partial clones; use 'git log -1 --no-walk'. (PR #372 review)\" --path constraints.git.invariant --branch main --json 2>&1 | grep -E '\"id\"' | head -1","description":"Retract the wrong 2.45 fact and record the corrected one"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
